<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mihira Bhoja</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 836–885</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Bhoja I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mihira Bhoja (Bhoja I) was the most powerful king of the Gurjara-Pratihara dynasty, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> from about 836 to 885. He rebuilt Pratihara power after earlier defeats and ruled an empire from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> to the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, contending with the Pala and Rashtrakuta dynasties in the tripartite struggle. Arab travellers praised his cavalry and the security of his realm.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mihira-bhoja.jpg" alt="Mihira Bhoja" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Ancientcoinsofindia1, English Wikipedia · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pratihara_bhoja.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
